  <wpt lat="39.797964" lon="-105.776804">
    <ele>3448.1</ele>
    <name>Winter Park (access: Berthoud Pass (US 40))</name>
    <desc>mi 1714.4 (SOBO mi 1714.4) | off. mi 1719.8 | CO | CO map 183 | Leave the CDT here for Winter Park: 7 mi by hitching. CDTC Gateway Community (as Winter Park-Fraser). Fireside Market in Winter Park handles a resupply; the bigger Safeway is in Fraser two miles north, and the two towns work as one stop.</desc>
    <type>town</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="39.925549" lon="-105.786695">
    <ele>2776.7</ele>
    <name>Winter Park</name>
    <desc>mi 1714.4 (SOBO mi 1714.4) | off. mi 1719.8 | CO | CO map 183 | CDTC Gateway Community (as Winter Park-Fraser). Fireside Market in Winter Park handles a resupply; the bigger Safeway is in Fraser two miles north, and the two towns work as one stop. Reached from Berthoud Pass on US 40, about 7 miles north - a busy road and an easy hitch. A free shuttle links Winter Park and Fraser. Accepts resupply boxes. Post office: 78490 US Highway 40, Winter Park, CO 80482 (general delivery). 7 mi off trail by hitching. Checked 2026-09.</desc>
    <type>town</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="39.797964" lon="-105.776804">
    <ele>3448.1</ele>
    <name>Fraser (access: Berthoud Pass (US 40))</name>
    <desc>mi 1714.4 (SOBO mi 1714.4) | off. mi 1719.8 | CO | CO map 183 | Leave the CDT here for Fraser: 10 mi by hitching. The full-supermarket half of the Winter Park-Fraser Gateway Community: Safeway on CR 804, two miles north of Winter Park and linked by a free shuttle. Cheaper than anything in the ski village.</desc>
    <type>town</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="39.945643" lon="-105.811125">
    <ele>2613.4</ele>
    <name>Fraser</name>
    <desc>mi 1714.4 (SOBO mi 1714.4) | off. mi 1719.8 | CO | CO map 183 | The full-supermarket half of the Winter Park-Fraser Gateway Community: Safeway on CR 804, two miles north of Winter Park and linked by a free shuttle. Cheaper than anything in the ski village. Accepts resupply boxes. Post office: 520 Zerex Street, Fraser, CO 80442 (general delivery). 10 mi off trail by hitching. Checked 2026-09.</desc>
    <type>town</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="39.794354" lon="-105.863394">
    <ele>3622.2</ele>
    <name>Official mile 1310</name>
    <desc>mi 1722.5 (SOBO mi 1722.5) | off. mi 1727.9 | CO | CO map 183 | CDTC official mile 1310, counted northbound from Crazy Cook. Official SOBO mile 1727.9; geometric SOBO mile 1722.5.</desc>
    <type>milestone</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="39.773672" lon="-105.889301">
    <ele>3796.3</ele>
    <name>Grays and Torreys / Argentine Spine branches</name>
    <desc>mi 1726.4 (SOBO mi 1726.4) | off. mi 1731.9 | CO | CO map 182 | Grays and Torreys / Argentine Spine: 45.3 mi against 80.8 mi on the CDT, +10,310 ft. The &apos;official route&apos; over Grays and Torreys Peaks and along the Argentine Spine, from CO 15 to CO 18 via detail sheets CO 15a-d, versus Ley&apos;s red line through Silverthorne. OSM relation 8067605 &apos;CDT ij- Sliverthorn Route&apos; (ref:xref &apos;CO ley=15-18, cdtc=xxx&apos;), 48.8 mi, both ends within 35 m of the primary line. Ley: &apos;It does go over some dramatic topography, but also some roads. The red route is nice too, and more consistently on trail. Resupply is a little easier on the red route as it goes through Silverthorne. But there are free busses along the Grays-Torreys route.&apos; CO 15a note 4: &apos;This route is a knife edge with lots of exposure and steep cliffs.&apos;</desc>
    <type>junction</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="39.721038" lon="-105.880595">
    <ele>3480.1</ele>
    <name>Official mile 1300</name>
    <desc>mi 1732.4 (SOBO mi 1732.4) | off. mi 1737.9 | CO | CO map 181 | CDTC official mile 1300, counted northbound from Crazy Cook. Official SOBO mile 1737.9; geometric SOBO mile 1732.4.</desc>
